\chapter{Checked source interfaces}
\label{ch:34}
\index{Checked source interfaces}
\index{Modules!interfaces}

An \OnxCodeTerm{.on1xi} v1 declaration describes the exact source signatures required of its paired implementation. Begin with \OnxCodeTerm{on1xi 1;} and use explicit function signatures. Parameter names can differ between interface and implementation. Parameter order, tuple shape, and result type must agree. V1 permits \OnxCodeTerm{i64}, Boolean, string and their admitted nested tuples, with \OnxCodeTerm{void} allowed as the whole result.

\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none]
on1xi 1;
fn count(label: string) -> i64;
fn describe(n: i64) -> (string, bool);
\end{OnxCodeBlock}

Declare the interface through \OnxCodeTerm{On1xfile.i} for a project, or inspect the CLI's \OnxCodeTerm{--check-interfaces} mode with \OnxCodeTerm{--interface} inputs. The implementation needs local definitions with explicit type headers. Inferred headers, re-exports, and raw externs do not satisfy it. The check also validates the ordinary program and runs without PPX or declaration-extension execution. For a project test, drift an implementation from a flat tuple to a nested tuple and check that the signature mismatch is located. Export policy and binary compatibility require their own checks.

